<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ansfried of Utrecht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1010">1010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ansfried was a count in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> who served Emperor Otto III before becoming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Utrecht in 995. Around 990 he and his wife Hilsondis founded the convent of Thorn, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, with their daughter Benedicta as its first abbess. Going blind in later life, he retired to the abbey of Hohorst, which he had founded, and died there in 1010. He is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ansfried-of-utrecht.jpg" alt="Ansfried of Utrecht" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huy_051027_(38).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The main source for his life is the De diversitatem temporum of the Benedictine Alpert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, written around 1022. Ansfried appears to have been the son or grandson of Lambert, a nobleman of the Maasgau, and to have been related to the Ottonian royal family. He shared his name with a paternal uncle, Ansfried the elder, a count said to have held 15 counties, and studied secular and clerical subjects under another uncle, Robert, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, before attending the cathedral school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 961 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> took him into his service as sword-bearer, and at Otto's imperial coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the following year Ansfried was told to keep the sword close at hand against any danger. As Count of Huy he held rich possessions along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, in Brabant and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a>, and he had considerable success in suppressing piracy and armed robbery. In 985 Otto III granted him the right to mint coins at Medemblik, on the shipping route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlie">Vlie</a>, together with tolls and taxes. The abbey church he founded on his wife's estate at Thorn was dedicated to Saint Michael and held directly of the Emperor.</p><p>Widowed, he wished to become a monk, but Otto III and Bishop Notker of Liège persuaded him to take the see of Utrecht. He protested that a man who had borne weapons was unworthy, then laid his sword on the altar of Saint Mary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and was ordained priest and consecrated in the same ceremony. Unlike Notker and the Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, he never took a command in the royal army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The abbey he founded in 1006 was also dedicated to Saint Michael, and near the end of his life, weakened by fasting and almost blind, he cared for the sick there as a monk. After his death the people of that place seized his body, while the people of Utrecht were putting out a fire. The abbess of Thorn mediated, and he was buried in the Cathedral of Saint Martin in Utrecht. His feast, once 3 May, was moved to 11 May, and he is the patron saint of Amersfoort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ansfried of Utrecht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The abbey was founded around 990 by Count <b>Ansfried</b>, later bishop of Utrecht, and his wife Hilsondis, with their daughter Benedicta as first abbess.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-thorn">Imperial Abbey of Thorn</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Leusden, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.13,5.43&amp;year=1000" aria-label="Leusden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4672 363 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ansfried of Utrecht"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5122 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5122 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.13,5.43&amp;year=1000">What was here in 1000? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
